1
00:00:02,395 --> 00:00:06,950
This is a projection that follows a moving face.

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:08,430
A Kinect depth camera watches.

3
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:10,990
A small Optoma projector paints.

4
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:14,095
This video explains the geometry that links them:

5
00:00:14,095 --> 00:00:16,930
how a camera pixel becomes a point in the room,

6
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:19,431
and how that point becomes a projector pixel.

7
00:00:21,538 --> 00:00:23,838
Two pieces of equipment.

8
00:00:23,838 --> 00:00:26,643
The Kinect holds two sensors.

9
00:00:26,643 --> 00:00:30,503
A colour camera, 1920 by 1080 pixels.

10
00:00:30,503 --> 00:00:35,483
And an infrared depth camera, 512 by 424 pixels,

11
00:00:35,483 --> 00:00:38,899
which measures distance by timing its own light.

12
00:00:38,899 --> 00:00:43,919
The projector is a small Optoma, 1920 by 1080 pixels,

13
00:00:43,919 --> 00:00:46,702
connected to the computer as a second screen.

14
00:00:48,768 --> 00:00:51,798
Here is the rig from above.

15
00:00:51,798 --> 00:00:54,433
The Kinect sits at the origin.

16
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:57,098
The projector stands to its side.

17
00:00:57,098 --> 00:01:00,808
The distance between them is the baseline, b.

18
00:01:00,808 --> 00:01:03,708
The person is at a distance d in front,

19
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:05,844
and a wall is further back.

20
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:08,579
The two devices never share a viewpoint,

21
00:01:08,579 --> 00:01:12,569
so a pixel in the camera is not a pixel in the projector.

22
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:15,399
The only common ground is the room itself.

23
00:01:17,562 --> 00:01:19,642
Three images are involved,

24
00:01:19,642 --> 00:01:22,887
so there are three sets of pixel coordinates.

25
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:28,147
x and y in the depth camera, with a distance d at every pixel.

26
00:01:28,147 --> 00:01:31,217
x prime and y prime in the colour camera.

27
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:34,907
x double prime and y double prime in the projector.

28
00:01:34,907 --> 00:01:38,542
In every image, x counts columns to the right,

29
00:01:38,542 --> 00:01:40,767
and y counts rows downward.

30
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:45,352
The room has one set of coordinates: capital X, Y and Z,

31
00:01:45,352 --> 00:01:48,396
in metres, measured from the colour camera.

32
00:01:48,396 --> 00:01:51,766
X to the right, Y up, and Z straight ahead.

33
00:01:53,882 --> 00:01:56,917
How does a pixel become a point?

34
00:01:56,917 --> 00:01:59,072
A pixel names a direction:

35
00:01:59,072 --> 00:02:03,857
a ray that leaves the lens centre and passes through that pixel.

36
00:02:03,857 --> 00:02:05,592
The focal length, f,

37
00:02:05,592 --> 00:02:08,752
is the distance from the lens centre to the image,

38
00:02:08,752 --> 00:02:10,577
counted in pixels.

39
00:02:10,577 --> 00:02:15,882
Take the pixel's column x prime, and subtract the image centre.

40
00:02:15,882 --> 00:02:18,322
Divide by f, and you have the slope of the ray.

41
00:02:18,322 --> 00:02:22,142
Multiply by the distance Z, and you have the sideways position,

42
00:02:22,142 --> 00:02:24,295
capital X, in metres.

43
00:02:24,295 --> 00:02:27,435
The rows work the same way and give capital Y,

44
00:02:27,435 --> 00:02:28,615
with a minus sign,

45
00:02:28,615 --> 00:02:32,035
because rows count downward while Y points up.

46
00:02:32,035 --> 00:02:35,457
So a pixel plus a distance is a point in the room.

47
00:02:37,502 --> 00:02:40,402
But the distance was measured by the other lens,

48
00:02:40,402 --> 00:02:42,982
five centimetres to the side.

49
00:02:42,982 --> 00:02:46,727
How does a depth pixel find its colour pixel?

50
00:02:46,727 --> 00:02:51,052
First, the depth pixel x, y, with its distance d,

51
00:02:51,052 --> 00:02:53,407
is lifted to a point in the room,

52
00:02:53,407 --> 00:02:56,437
using the depth camera's own focal length.

53
00:02:56,437 --> 00:02:57,277
Second,

54
00:02:57,277 --> 00:03:00,887
the point is shifted by the offset between the two lenses,

55
00:03:00,887 --> 00:03:03,077
which was measured at the factory.

56
00:03:03,077 --> 00:03:06,327
Third, the point is projected into the colour camera.

57
00:03:06,327 --> 00:03:10,047
That gives the colour pixel, x prime, y prime,

58
00:03:10,047 --> 00:03:11,974
that sees the same spot.

59
00:03:11,974 --> 00:03:15,344
The shift between the two images depends on distance:

60
00:03:15,344 --> 00:03:18,084
near things shift more than far things.

61
00:03:18,084 --> 00:03:19,814
Done for every depth pixel,

62
00:03:19,814 --> 00:03:22,972
this gives every colour pixel its own distance.

63
00:03:24,985 --> 00:03:27,455
The result is a point X, Y,

64
00:03:27,455 --> 00:03:31,805
Z for every colour pixel that has a distance.

65
00:03:31,805 --> 00:03:35,160
This is the real data from the rig.

66
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:39,300
The depth cloud of the room, with the Kinect in cyan,

67
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:41,520
and the projector in magenta,

68
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,253
drawn where the calibration placed it.

69
00:03:46,292 --> 00:03:48,242
Now the projector.

70
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:50,402
It is a camera running backwards:

71
00:03:50,402 --> 00:03:53,517
light leaves through the lens where a camera would take it in.

72
00:03:53,517 --> 00:03:55,337
So it has the same description.

73
00:03:55,337 --> 00:03:58,622
A focal length, an image centre, and a pose:

74
00:03:58,622 --> 00:04:01,452
a rotation R and a translation t,

75
00:04:01,452 --> 00:04:04,307
which say where it sits relative to the Kinect.

76
00:04:04,307 --> 00:04:05,867
Take a point in the room.

77
00:04:05,867 --> 00:04:09,132
Rotate and translate it into the projector's own frame.

78
00:04:09,132 --> 00:04:12,412
Divide by its distance, multiply by the focal length,

79
00:04:12,412 --> 00:04:14,517
and add the image centre.

80
00:04:14,517 --> 00:04:17,072
The result is x double prime, y double prime:

81
00:04:17,072 --> 00:04:19,396
the projector pixel that lights that point.

82
00:04:21,555 --> 00:04:23,405
To find the pose,

83
00:04:23,405 --> 00:04:27,240
the projector has to show where its pixels land.

84
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:32,505
It draws a grid of dots, each at a known pixel, x double prime,

85
00:04:32,505 --> 00:04:34,415
y double prime.

86
00:04:34,415 --> 00:04:39,915
Over a sequence of frames, every dot blinks its own binary code.

87
00:04:39,915 --> 00:04:43,835
When the camera sees a dot at x prime, y prime,

88
00:04:43,835 --> 00:04:47,385
the code says which projector pixel it came from.

89
00:04:47,385 --> 00:04:50,842
And the depth gives that dot's position in the room.

90
00:04:50,842 --> 00:04:54,882
So each dot is one pair: a point X, Y, Z,

91
00:04:54,882 --> 00:04:57,132
and the projector pixel that lit it.

92
00:04:59,508 --> 00:05:02,968
The model has seven unknowns.

93
00:05:02,968 --> 00:05:05,933
Three for the rotation, three for the translation,

94
00:05:05,933 --> 00:05:08,243
and the focal length.

95
00:05:08,243 --> 00:05:12,998
Each pair gives two equations: one for x double prime,

96
00:05:12,998 --> 00:05:15,183
one for y double prime.

97
00:05:15,183 --> 00:05:18,743
A few hundred pairs are far more than enough,

98
00:05:18,743 --> 00:05:22,848
so the fit looks for the values that bring the model's dots

99
00:05:22,848 --> 00:05:26,328
closest to the dots the projector really drew,

100
00:05:26,328 --> 00:05:30,202
and it ignores dots that were decoded wrongly.

101
00:05:30,202 --> 00:05:35,257
Written as one matrix, the projector is K times R, t:

102
00:05:35,257 --> 00:05:39,657
three rows, four columns, taking room points to pixels.

103
00:05:41,812 --> 00:05:44,247
Why does the focal length matter?

104
00:05:44,247 --> 00:05:46,392
It sets the magnification.

105
00:05:46,392 --> 00:05:49,502
An object of size s, at distance D,

106
00:05:49,502 --> 00:05:52,862
covers f times s over D pixels.

107
00:05:52,862 --> 00:05:55,302
A face fifteen centimetres wide,

108
00:05:55,302 --> 00:05:57,732
ninety centimetres from the projector,

109
00:05:57,732 --> 00:06:00,507
covers about three hundred and seventy pixels.

110
00:06:00,507 --> 00:06:02,802
If the model's focal length is wrong,

111
00:06:02,802 --> 00:06:05,735
the image on the face is the wrong size.

112
00:06:07,822 --> 00:06:09,907
And there is a trap.

113
00:06:09,907 --> 00:06:12,467
If every dot lands on the wall,

114
00:06:12,467 --> 00:06:16,212
the fit cannot tell a short focal length with the projector

115
00:06:16,212 --> 00:06:19,682
close from a long one with the projector far back.

116
00:06:19,682 --> 00:06:22,447
Both draw the same dots on the wall.

117
00:06:22,447 --> 00:06:25,262
They only disagree on a near object.

118
00:06:25,262 --> 00:06:28,182
So the scan needs dots on something near,

119
00:06:28,182 --> 00:06:30,572
such as the person sitting in the beam,

120
00:06:30,572 --> 00:06:33,304
and those dots pin the focal length.

121
00:06:35,378 --> 00:06:37,918
Now the moving target.

122
00:06:37,918 --> 00:06:40,338
In every camera frame,

123
00:06:40,338 --> 00:06:43,808
MediaPipe finds 468 landmarks on the face,

124
00:06:43,808 --> 00:06:47,453
as colour pixels x prime, y prime.

125
00:06:47,453 --> 00:06:48,678
That is the shape.

126
00:06:48,678 --> 00:06:50,638
The Kinect adds the distance,

127
00:06:50,638 --> 00:06:54,453
and the depth at the landmarks sets how deep the relief is.

128
00:06:54,453 --> 00:06:58,168
So each landmark becomes a point X, Y, Z.

129
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:00,569
Each point goes through the projector model.

130
00:07:00,569 --> 00:07:03,644
That gives the mesh as the projector must draw it.

131
00:07:03,644 --> 00:07:06,606
Triangles that face away from the projector are not drawn.

132
00:07:08,718 --> 00:07:12,723
How does a flat painting end up on a curved face?

133
00:07:12,723 --> 00:07:16,098
Every point of the mesh has a second address:

134
00:07:16,098 --> 00:07:20,453
a fixed place on a flat, square image, the texture.

135
00:07:20,453 --> 00:07:22,593
That layout is the U V map.

136
00:07:22,593 --> 00:07:24,833
It is the face, unfolded.

137
00:07:24,833 --> 00:07:27,528
In Blender, the paint goes onto the mesh,

138
00:07:27,528 --> 00:07:29,389
and lands in that square.

139
00:07:29,389 --> 00:07:32,764
At run time, the triangles are the same ones.

140
00:07:32,764 --> 00:07:35,689
Each triangle of the square is stretched onto the matching

141
00:07:35,689 --> 00:07:37,559
triangle of the live mesh,

142
00:07:37,559 --> 00:07:39,413
in the projector's image.

143
00:07:39,413 --> 00:07:42,287
Only the corners move, and the paint follows.

144
00:07:44,335 --> 00:07:46,650
Where does error come from?

145
00:07:46,650 --> 00:07:50,110
Mostly from depth, through the baseline.

146
00:07:50,110 --> 00:07:54,645
Suppose the measured distance is too long by delta Z.

147
00:07:54,645 --> 00:07:58,095
The system then believes the point lies further along the

148
00:07:58,095 --> 00:07:59,660
camera's ray.

149
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:03,245
It aims the projector at that believed point.

150
00:08:03,245 --> 00:08:07,520
But the light stops at the real surface, before it gets there.

151
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,935
It lands beside the true point.

152
00:08:09,935 --> 00:08:14,645
The sideways shift is about delta Z, times the baseline b,

153
00:08:14,645 --> 00:08:16,645
over the distance d.

154
00:08:16,645 --> 00:08:19,355
With the projector seventy centimetres away,

155
00:08:19,355 --> 00:08:24,792
one centimetre of depth error moves the image nine millimetres.

156
00:08:24,792 --> 00:08:26,177
Slide the projector next to the Kinect,

157
00:08:26,177 --> 00:08:29,021
and the same error moves it about one millimetre.

158
00:08:31,208 --> 00:08:35,898
Put together, this is a feedback loop around a moving object.

159
00:08:35,898 --> 00:08:37,203
The face moves.

160
00:08:37,203 --> 00:08:39,383
The colour camera captures it.

161
00:08:39,383 --> 00:08:43,123
MediaPipe finds the landmarks: that is the target.

162
00:08:43,123 --> 00:08:46,628
The depth camera attaches a distance to each landmark,

163
00:08:46,628 --> 00:08:48,684
which makes it a point in the room.

164
00:08:48,684 --> 00:08:52,249
The projector model turns the point into a projector pixel,

165
00:08:52,249 --> 00:08:54,339
and light lands on the face.

166
00:08:54,339 --> 00:08:57,019
Then the face moves again, and the loop runs again,

167
00:08:57,019 --> 00:08:58,784
fifteen times a second.

168
00:08:58,784 --> 00:09:00,969
One pass takes a fraction of a second,

169
00:09:00,969 --> 00:09:03,629
so the system aims at where the face will be when the light

170
00:09:03,629 --> 00:09:04,572
arrives.

171
00:09:06,685 --> 00:09:11,565
Camera pixel, to a point in the room, to a projector pixel,

172
00:09:11,565 --> 00:09:13,485
and back onto the face.

